 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(H \cdot W \cdot k)$, where $H, W$ are image dimensions and $k$ is the number of neighbors. The KNN search is simplified by using a fixed spatial window.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(H \cdot W)$ to store the depth maps.
-*   **Strengths:** Effectively enforces local geometric consistency, reducing noise and artifacts in sparse-view reconstruction [S1]. It preserves sharp edges better than global smoothing methods by using a boundary tolerance [S1, 10369-patch-based-depth-regularization.md].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the external depth prior (e.g., DepthAnythingV2). If the external depth is inaccurate, the regularization may introduce errors. Computationally more expensive than simple pixel-wise L1/L2 depth losses due to the neighbor aggregation [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** Sparse-view 3D reconstruction where depth information is ambiguous or noisy [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Dense-view scenarios where the NeRF can learn geometry accurately from multi-view consistency alone, or when external depth priors are unavailable or unreliable.
-
+*   Time complexity: $O(H \cdot W \cdot k)$, where $H, W$ are image dimensions and $k$ is the number of neighbors.
+*   Memory complexity: $O(H \cdot W)$ for storing depth maps.
+*   Strengths: Enforces structural consistency, handles minor variations via margin, improves sparse-view reconstruction quality [S1].
+*   Limitations: Depends on the quality of the prior depth map; KNN search can be computationally expensive if not optimized.
+*   When to use: Sparse-view NeRF training where depth continuity is critical and a reliable prior depth map is available [S1].
 ## Background
-Depth Structural Consistency Regularization was introduced in the context of DASNeRF (Depth consistency optimization, Adaptive sampling, and Hierarchical structural fusion for sparse view neural radiance fields) [S1]. It leverages modern monocular depth estimation models like DepthAnythingV2 to provide strong geometric priors for NeRF training, particularly in sparse-view settings [S1]. The method aims to clarify depth information and enhance scene reconstruction quality by enforcing continuity constraints derived from these priors [S1].
-
+Originated in DASNeRF, which combines depth consistency optimization, adaptive sampling, and hierarchical structural fusion for sparse-view neural radiance fields [S1]. It leverages modern depth estimation models like DepthAnythingV2 to provide robust depth priors [S1].
